\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Pit Fiend:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 The most terrible baatezu of the Nine Hells, pit fiends appear to be gian
t, winged humanoids, very much like gargoyles in appearance, with huge wings that wrap around their bodies for defense. Pit fiend's fangs are large and drip with a vile, green liquid. Their bodies are red and scaly, often emitting flames when they are ang
ered or excited. In the rare situations they choose to communicate, they use telepathy.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Combat:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 In physical combat, the pit fiend is capable of dealing out tremendous punishment, using its incredible 18/00 Strength (+6 damage adjustment). They can attack s
ix times in a single round, dividing attacks against six different opponents. They can attack with two hard, scaly wing buffets for 1-4 points of damage per hit. Their powerful claws do 1-6 points of damage per successful attack. The bite of a pit fiend i
s dreadful indeed, causing any creature bitten to take 2-12 points of damage and receive a lethal dose of poison. A saving throw vs. poison is required or the victim dies in 1-4 rounds. The bite also infects the victim with a disease.
\par \tab Pit fiends can also a
ttack with their tail every round, inflicting 2-8 points of damage per hit. The tail can then hold and constrict the victim for 2-8 points of damage per round until the victim makes a successful Strength check to break free. Pit fiends can also carry jagg
ed-toothed clubs which inflict 7-12 points of damage per hit (this replaces one claw attack).
\par \tab Once per round a pit fiend can use one of the following spell-like powers, plus those available to all baatezu: }{\i\fs24 detect magic, detect invisibility, fireball, hold person, improved invisibility, polymorph self, produce flame, pyrotechnics,}{
\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 wall of fire.
\par }{\fs24 \tab They can, once per year, cast a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  spell. They may always }{\i\fs24 gate}{\fs24  in two lesser or one greater baatezu with a 10
0% chance of success, performing this action once per round. Once per day, a pit fiend can use a }{\i\fs24 symbol}{\fs24 
 of pain -- the victim must save vs. rod, staff or wand or suffer a -4 penalty on attack dice, and a -2 penalty to Dexterity for 2-20 rounds.
\par \tab Pit fiends regenerate 2 hit points per round and radiate a }{\i\fs24 fear}{\fs24  aura in a 20-foot radius (save vs. rod, staff, or wand at a -3 penalty or flee in panic for 1-10 rounds).
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Habitat/Society:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Pit fiends are the lords of the Nine Hells. They are the baatezu of the greate
st power and the highest station. Pit fiends are found throughout the various layers of the Nine Hells, but are very rare on the upper layers.
\par \tab Wherever they are found, these mighty lords hold a position of great authority and power. They sometimes command
 vast legions consisting of dozens of complete armies, leading them into battle against the tanar'ri. These huge forces are terrifying to behold, and any non-native of the lower planes, of less than 10 Hit Dice, who sees them, flees in panic for 1-3 days.
 Those of 10 Hit Dice or more must make a saving throw vs. rod, staff, or wand or flee in panic for 1-12 turns.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Ecology:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Pit fiends are spawned from the powerful gelugons of the Nine Hells' eighth layer. When those icy fiends are found worthy they are cast into the Pit of Flame for 1,001 days after which they emerge as pit fiends.

\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Abishai:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Abishai are common on the first and second layers of the Nine Hells, appearing much like gothic gargoyles. They are thin and reptilian with long, prehensile tails and gr
eat wings. There are three varieties of abishai. They are, in ascending order of station, black, green, and red. Abishai communicate with telepathy.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Combat:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 In battle, the abishai strikes with formidable claws, inflicting 1-4 points of damage per successf
ul hit. It can also lash out with its flexible tail for 2-5 points of damage. Hidden in the end of an abishai's tail is a small stinger that injects poison on a successful hit, requiring a saving throw vs. poison (failure results in death).
\par \tab Abishai can fly into the air and dive at enemies, striking with both claws. Their attack roll is made with a +2 bonus. A successful hit inflicts double damage.
\par \tab In addition to the powers possessed by all baatezu, an abishai can perform the following magical powers, one at a time, once per round: }{\i\fs24 change self, command, produce flame, pyrotechnics,}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 scare.}{\fs24 
 They can also attempt to}{\i\fs24  gate}{\fs24  2-12 lemures (60% chance of success, once per day) or 1-3 abishai (30% chance of success, once per day).
\par \tab All abishai are susceptible to damage from holy water. If a vial is splashed on it, an abishai suffers 2-8 points of damage. All abishai regenerate 1 hit point per melee round unless the damage was done by holy water or a holy magical weapon.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Habitat/Society:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Abishai are voracious and evil. They delight in tormenting those few natives of the Nine Hells that are lower in power. Abishai are fond of using }{\i\fs24 change self}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 charm person}{
\fs24  to tempt mortals bold enough to travel to the Nine Hells.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Ecology:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 The abishai comprise the main body of m
any large, evil armies battling against the tanar'ri and intruders against the Nine Hells. In some cases, a red abishai may have proven himself worthy enough to command a force of lemures. If it is successful in this endeavor, the red abishai may be promo
ted to a higher form of baatezu.
\par 
