\par 
\par \tab While the gauth is similar to the beholder, its ability to feed on the energy of magical objects makes it even more dangerous in some ways.
\par \tab When a gauth moves into combat, it begins to glow, much as if it were the object of a }{\i\fs24 faerie fire}{\fs24  spell, to attract the attention of its foes. A creature that meets the}{\i\fs24  gaze}{\fs24 
 of the central eye must roll a successful saving throw vs. spell, with a -2 penalty, or be affected as if the victim of a }{\i\fs24 feeblemind}{\fs24  spell.
\par \tab If a gauth chooses to bite with its great maw, the sharp fangs inflict 3d4 points of damage. The four tendrils around the mouth can grab and hold victims as if they had a Strength of 18, but they can inflict no damage.
\par \tab A gauth in combat can also employ its six eye stalks. These eyes have the following powers:
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\adjustright {\fs24 1. }{\i\fs24 Cause serious wounds}{\fs24  (as spell, 30-foot range).
\par 2. }{\i\fs24 Repulsion}{\fs24  (as spell, 10-foot wide path, 40-foot range)
\par 3. }{\i\fs24 Cone of cold}{\fs24  (as spell, inflicts 3d4 points of damage and has an area of effect 5 feet wide at the start, 50 feet long, and 20 feet wide at the base; this eye can be used only three times per day)
\par 4. }{\i\fs24 Lightning bolt}{\fs24  (as spell, inflicts 4d4 damage with 80' range; this power can be used up to four times per day)
\par 5. }{\i\fs24 Paralyzation}{\fs24  (as wand, 40-foot range, single target; only a }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  or the beholder's death can free the victim)
\par 6. }{\i\fs24 Dweomer drain}{\fs24  (see below)
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-1080\tx-720\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx3600\tx4860\tx5760\tx6390\tx7200\tx7740\tx8820\tx9000\tx10080\tx10800\tx11520\tx12240\tx12960\adjustright {\fs24 \tab 
Perhaps the most feared of the gauth's powers, its dweomer drain, permits the gauth to drain charges from magical items. It has a 40-foot range and can be targeted on one individual per round. I
n addition to preventing one object from functioning for the duration of that round, this power drains one charge from one charged object. Permanent objects, such as magical swords, are rendered powerless for one round by this ability. Artifacts are not a
ffected by the }{\i\fs24 dweomer drain.}{\fs24  The eye has no effect on spells that have been memorized (but not yet cast) and it will not break the concentration of a wizard. It does neutralize any spell cast by its target that round, however.
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  spell cast on
 any of the gauth's eye stalks prevents its use for 1d4 rounds. The central eye, any fully retracted eye stalks, the body's ability to glow, and the gauth's natural levitation are not subject to injury by such a spell.
\par \tab If a gauth is slain, its magical ene
rgy dissipates. Usually, this is a harmless event, but there is a 2% chance that it is catastrophic, inflicting 4d4 points of damage to all creatures within 10 feet (no saving throw). Gauth are immune to their own powers and to those of other gauth. They 
have an unusual physiology that enables them to regenerate 1 hit point every two turns.
\par \tab Although gauth are not known to fight over territories or prey, they do go to great lengths to avoid each other. Even when they encounter another of their kind in the wilderness, they often ignore them utterly.
\par \tab A gauth can survive by eating meat but it greatly prefers to devour magical objects. In some unknown manner, the creature is able to absorb magical energy and feed on it. Each turn that an object spends in the ga
uth's stomach causes it to lose one charge. A permanent object is rendered inoperative after one day (artifacts are not affected, nor do they provide sustenance). Magical objects that cannot be entirely digested by a gauth are spat out after they have bee
n drained of all their power.
\par \tab Gauth are thought to live a century or so. Within a week of their ``natural'' death, two young gauth emerge from the corpse. Although smaller than their parent (each has 2+2 or 3+3 HD and a bite that causes only 2d4 points of 
damage), they have all the powers of a full-grown adult. 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Spectator}{\b\fs24  (beholder-kin)
\par }{\fs24 Another relative of the beholder, the spectator is a guardian of places and treasures, and capable of limited planar travel. Once it is given a task, the spectator will wat
ch for up to 101 years. It will allow no one to use, borrow, or examine an item or treasure, except the one who gave it its orders. The spectator has a large central eye and four smaller eye stalks protruding from the top of its hovering, spherical body.

\par \tab The spectator is difficult to surprise, and has a +2 surprise modifier and a +1 initiative modifier. It is basically a passive creature, and will attempt to communicate and implant }{\i\fs24 suggestion}{\fs24 
 as its first act, unless it is immediately attacked. Striking a spectator has the following effects:
\par 
\par 
