\par }\pard\plain \s20\li115\ri130\sa48\nowidctlpar\tx-1080\tx180\tx1170\tx1530\tx2070\tx2430\tx3690\tx4230\tx5220\tx5850\tx6210\tx7290\tx7650\tx8910\tx9450\tx10800\tx11520\tx12240\adjustright \cgrid {
Mammals are the most common of the vertebrate. Generally born alive, mammals possess hair. Warm-blooded, mammals maintain a single body temperatu
re, regulated by an internal thermostat. Most mammals are harmless to humanoids and they are often trained to perform a specific function.
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx-1080\tx180\tx1170\tx1530\tx2070\tx2430\tx3690\tx4230\tx5220\tx5850\tx6210\tx7290\tx7650\tx8910\tx9450\tx10800\tx11520\tx12240\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Ape, Carnivorous:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Carnivorous apes gain a +2 to their surprise rolls. They eat anything they can kill.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Baboon, Wild:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Wild baboons are large, herbivorous primates that are characterized by long arms and legs, large dog-like muzzles, and sharp canine teeth.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Badger:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Badgers are vicious little creatures that prefer to run from danger than fight. If cornered, the badger will fight, attempting to bite the tender throat of its opponent.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Banderlog:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Banderlogs resemble baboons with green skin and brown fur. They throw retch plant fruit or coconuts at enemies. Coconuts cause 1d4+1 points damage while retch plant fru
it splash a 5-foot radius area with nauseating fluids. Those caught in the splash, retch for three rounds and their Strength is reduced by half for the next hour+no saving throw allowed.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Bhaergala:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 This predator jumps its prey from great heights. It can d
rop from as high as 60 feet, causing maximum damage plus 1d6 for every 10 feet dropped. This beast heals 2 hit points every day and gains a +3 to all saving throws vs. poison. Four times a day, the bhaergala can turn spells as the }{\i\fs24 
ring of spell turning}{\fs24 .
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Boar, Wild:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Wild boar continue to attack until they are reduced to -7 hit points. The giant boar is often called an alothere.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Chattur:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Chatturs, called space bandits, are small mammals that exhibit traits of both primates and rodents. Not very combative, they fight in defense of their nests, kin, or friends.
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Cooshee:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Known as elven dogs, they only bark to warn their elven masters of danger. They can strike bipedal opponents with front paws to knock man-sized or smaller creatures down.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Dakon:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Dakons are intelligent apes with a reasonable grasp of the common tongue. When attacking it gains a +2 bonus.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Debbi:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 This desert scavenger, a baboon-hyena crossbreed, can }{\i\fs24 cause fear}{\fs24  by stamping its feet in a slow, rhythm and calling forth magical
 power that makes its fur crackle and spark. This fear lasts for two rounds.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Goat:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 When a goat charges, it gains a +2 attack bonus and does an additional 1-2 points damage.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Gorilla:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 The gorilla is non-aggressive and shy, but fights fiercely when threatened or cornered.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Hsing-sing:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 The hsing-sing are a recluse ape-like race believed to personify the principles of pacifism and harmony with nature. However, once a year, for 2d6+4 days, 
the males gather into raiding bands and complete the ``war season.'' Their bands attack humanoid settlements on the edge of their territory. Such attacks are impartial; pillaging good and evil creatures alike. To maximize the advantage of surprise, the hs
ing-sing rarely attack the same settlement two years in a row.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Hyena:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Often confused as a canine, the hyena taunts enemies with its nervous, high-pitched howl.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Jackal:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Jackals are timid scavengers that run from the threat of other predators. When attackin
g, the jackal darts in to bite its victim and quickly retreats to a safe distance. If more than one jackal is trying to down an animal, they attack in a haphazard fashion with little or no coordination of effort.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Losel:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 An arboreal orc/baboon cross, losel resemble primitive humans in many respects. They can throw stones up to 20 yards for 1d4 points damage.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Monkey Spider:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Measuring 1-inch in length, monkey spiders fight creatures their own size, avoiding combat with larger creatures. If forced to battle s
uch creatures, the monkeys jump and bite at an eye. The bite causes extreme pain and the victim must hold his eye and take no other action for a round, giving the monkey a chance to run away. Monkey spiders can jump 10 feet down or 5 feet forward and can 
climb anything a typical spider can.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Porcupine:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Porcupines are covered with long barbed quills. The creature is unable to throw its quills. Generally, a predator who chases the fleeing creature gets a mouthful of quills which causes the damage cited above.

\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Roth\'e9:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 The roth\'e9 (pronounced ``roth-AY'') is a musk ox-like creature with an aversion to light. It prefers to live underground near lichen and moss.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Skunk:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Anyone within a skunk's 10x10x10-foot cloud of musk must save vs. poison or be nauseated for 1-4 rounds, losing 50% of Strength and Dexterity.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Sleek:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 This ermine-like mammal surprises opponents on 1-5 on 1d6. On an attack roll of 19 or 20, the sleek opens a major vein, causing 2d6 points of damage. Every round thereafter, the victim suffers 1d6 more 
points damage until the wound is wrapped with a tourniquet, direct pressure, or magical healing.
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Stag, Wild:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Wild stags are the aggressive males of the deer herds. Normally docile and passive, they defend their herds against all but the most fearsome opponents.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Stench Kow:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 
Stench Kine are the cattle of the lower planes. They are immune to all forms of cold, fire, and poison. In close quarters, their breath and bodies are so foul, a save vs. poison must be made or the character is affected as if struck by a }{\i\fs24 
stinking cloud}{\fs24  spell. The save must be made every three rounds of exposure.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Taer:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 These vile primates live in cold mountain caves. They throw spears before closing in for hand-to-hand.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Tyrg:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 
When in melee, tyrgs emit fierce howls that mildly stun their victims (+2 penalty to initiative and -2 penalty to attack rolls) for the next three rounds+no saving throw allowed. These are tiger-dog crossbreeds that move silently 75% of the time and have 
a +2 bonus to avoid surprise.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Warthog:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 The warthog is so aggressive, it continues to attack until it reduced to -7 hit points.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Weasel:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Weasels, related to minks and stoats, are common predators, though they are hunted for their pelts, or for pets.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Wolverine:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 When engaged in battle, the wolverine becomes a most fearsome adversary, giving it a +4 bonus to its attack roll.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Minimal Mammals:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Minimals are half-sized breeds of otherwise normal animals. They have the same physical and behavioral traits of their 
full-sized relatives, although most are not dangerous. Minimals generally have one-half the number of Hit Dice with a corresponding drop in THAC0, a +2 Armor Class penalty, and a -2 Morale penalty. The damage caused by a minimal is one-half that of the fu
ll-sized relative.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Giant Mammals:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 
Giant mammals are double-sized breeds of otherwise normal animals. They have the same physical and behavioral traits of their full-sized relatives. These giants generally have double the number of Hit Dice, a corresponding
 rise in THAC0, a -2 AC bonus, and a +2 Morale bonus. The damage caused by the attacks of a giant mammal is twice that of the full-sized relative.
\par   
\par 
