\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Maedar
\par }{\fs24 Maedar are male counterparts to medusae, though they lack the petrifying gaze of the females.
\par \tab The typical maedar is a muscular, hairless humanoid male, usually dressed in a kilt or tunic.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Combat:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Maedar attack with powerful fists that each cause 2d4 points of damage. One every three turns, the maedar can turn stone to flesh by touch.
 Maedar can pass through stone at their normal movement rate. They require one round of concentration before this; no other activity can be undertaken that round. Maedar struck by a }{\i\fs24 phase door}{\fs24 
 spell while passing through stone are killed instantly.
\par \tab Maedar are immune to petrifaction and paralyzation (including related spells, such as }{\i\fs24 hold}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 slow}{\fs24 
). They are immune to the poisonous bite of a medusa's serpentine hair. In addition, maedar can see into and extend their stone into flesh power into the Astral and Ethereal planes.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Habitat/Society:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 
Maedar are the little-known male version of the medusae. They are extremely rare, however (far more rare than the frequency would indicate), and few medusae ever find a maedar spouse. Most medusae typically mate with hu
man males. This cross produces two to six eggs that hatch into fledgling, human-like females, who mature into medusae. The cross insures the continuation of the medusae species.
\par \tab 
When a medusa finds and mates with the extremely rare maedar, the eggs hatch into human infants, 25% male and 75% female. Only 1% of the males born of these matings are actually maedar; the remaining males and all the females are normal human infants who 
die at the sight of their mother.
\par \tab The typical maedar is a monogamist who mates for life; he is fiercely devoted to his mate and will go to any length to assist or avenge her. A widowed maedar will pursue his mate's killer for years.
\par \tab Because of the Maedar'
s rarity and natural reclusiveness, even most sages are unaware of their existence. Maedar are rarely seen; generally they remain in the lair they share with a medusa mate. A maedar's magical power provides food for him and his mate. He smashes her petrif
ied victims, then transforms them into meat.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Ecology:}{\fs24\cf9  }{\fs24 Maedar may cooperate with lawful evil creatures, such as kobolds and orcs, in exchange for security or reward. If forced to aid another creature, maedar seek revenge at the first opportunity.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Glyptar
\par }{\fs24 G
lyptars are rock crystals animated by the spirits of maedar. When a maedar has foreknowledge of his death, he can transfer his life force into the rocks. An extremely evil maedar retains his consciousness as it drifts through the ground. When such a life 
f
orce encounters gemstones, such as feldspar or amethyst, it is trapped within and cannot leave voluntarily. Eventually the maedar goes mad. (Note that once a maedar is trapped within a glyptar crystal, his life force cannot be trapped in a second crystal.
)
\par \tab 
If this glyptar is removed intact from the ground, the maedar's spirit is now able to animate the crystal and anything inorganic attached to it. Thus if the glyptar is set in the eye of a stone statue, the maedar's life force animates it as a golem. This
 can affect a maximum of 1,000 pounds.
\par \tab Similarly, if the glyptar is set in the pommel of a sword, the sword can be animated to strike as though it were wielded by the living maedar. The weapon gains a +1 bonus to its attack roll, strikes as if it were wie
lded by a 6th-level fighter, and gains a +2 bonus to its damage roll. Note that the glyptar's }{\i\fs24 stone to flesh}{\fs24  power enables the weapon to sink harmlessly into stone as the glyptar uses its power.
\par \tab The glyptar retains its other powers as well. The glyptar and its attachments may pass through stone at will at the normal movement rate. It is immune to }{\i\fs24 phase door}{\fs24  spells. Once every three turns the glyptar can perform a }{\i\fs24 
stone to flesh}{\fs24  attack against a target touched by the glyptar or its setting. Glyptars remain immune to paralyzation and petrification attacks of all sorts.
\par   
\par 
