\par 
\par Molds are a variety of spore-producing fungi that form in decaying food or in warm, moist places. These fungi usually have a woolly or furry texture. While most molds are harmless, there are (at least) three varieties of monstrous molds that pose a deadly
 threat to adventurers: brown, russet, and yellow mold.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Brown Mold
\par }{\fs24 Brown mold is found in damp subterranean areas, such as caverns and caves. It is light to golden brown in color. Brown mold feeds by absorbing heat, even body heat; where brown mold grows, 
the temperature is below average. Direct sunlight or ultraviolet light kills it.
\par \tab If a warm-blooded creature comes within 5 feet of a brown mold, the mold drains heat equal to 4d8 points of damage from its victim, per round. A }{\i\fs24 ring of warmth}{\fs24  provides compl
ete protection against this attack. Brown mold grows instantly from heat. If a torch is used in its vicinity, it doubles in size; if flaming oil is used, it quadruples, and fireball-type spells cause it to grow eight-fold.
\par \tab Brown mold is not fed by cold light sources (e.g., }{\i\fs24 light, faerie fire}{\fs24 ). The only magic that affects it are }{\i\fs24 disintegrate}{\fs24 
 (which destroys it), plant-affecting magic, and cold spells. Ice storms or walls of ice cause it to go dormant for 5d6 turns. A }{\i\fs24 cold wand}{\fs24 , white dragon breath, or a }{\i\fs24 cone of cold}{\fs24 
 kills it. Brown mold does not affect cold-using creatures such as white dragons, winter wolves, ice toads, etc.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Russet Mold
\par }{\fs24 Russet mold is golden-brown to rust red in color. It has a lumpy texture similar to cold porridge; it is covered by short
, hair-like growths that stand upright and wave as if they were in a cold breeze. It resembles rust at distances beyond 30 feet (70% chance of error). It is immune to weapons and most spells; it is affected only by alcohol, acid, and salt, which kill it; 
a }{\i\fs24 cure disease}{\fs24  or a }{\i\fs24 continual light}{\fs24  spell also destroys it.
\par \tab Russet mold continuously emits a cloud of spores in a three-foot radius. All creatures in this cloud suffer 5d4 points of damage (per round in the cloud) and must roll a successful saving throw vs
. poison or become infected with spore sickness. Victims of spore sickness are instantly paralyzed and die in 5d4 minutes unless a }{\i\fs24 cure disease}{\fs24  spell is cast on them.
\par \tab Anyone who dies from spore sickness undergoes a transformation and begins to sprout russet mold growths; when completely covered in mold (1d4+20 hours), he becomes a mold man or vegepygmy. A }{\i\fs24 hold plant}{\fs24 
 spell will halt the growth of the mold for the duration of the spell, while a }{\i\fs24 cure disease}{\fs24  spell destroys it within an hour after death; after that, a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  is necessary to destroy it.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Yellow Mold
\par }{\fs24 This mold is pale yellow to golden orange in color. If touched roughly, it may (50% chance) emit a cloud of spores in a 10-foot radius. Any creature caught in this cloud must roll a successful saving throw vs. poison or die. A }{\i\fs24 
cure disease}{\fs24  spell and a }{\i\fs24 resurrection}{\fs24  spell within 24 hours are necessary to restore life.
\par \tab Fire of any sort destroys yellow mold. A }{\i\fs24 continual light}{\fs24  spell renders it dormant for 2d6 turns.
\par \tab Yellow mold colonies of over 300 square feet are sometimes sentient (1 in 6 chance). These molds sense creatures within 60 feet, and may project their spores that distance. Twice per day, they may use a }{\i\fs24 suggestion}{\fs24 
 on someone within that radius; in addition to the saving throw, the victim must successfully roll an Intelligence check or lose 1 point of Intelligence permanently (it is devoured by the mold).
\par 
